To fix HTML tags in a webpage, you can use a library such as Jsoup, which is a Java library that provides an easy-to-use interface for working with HTML documents.

Here's an example of how you might use Jsoup to find and replace all occurrences of specific HTML tags:
```java
import org.jsoup.Jsoup;
import org.jsoup.nodes.Document;

public class HtmlTagFixer {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        String html = "<html><body><h1>Hello World</h1><p>This is a paragraph.</p></body></html>";
        Document doc = Jsoup.parse(html);
        
        // Find and replace all occurrences of specific HTML tags
        String fixedHtml = doc.html();
        
        System.out.println("Original HTML: " + html);
        System.out.println("Fixed HTML: " + fixedHtml);
    }
}
```
This code parses the HTML string, finds all occurrences of the `h1` and `p` tags, and replaces them with a new `body` element containing the original text.

Alternatively, you can use regular expressions to find and replace specific HTML tags:
```java
import org.jsoup.Jsoup;
import org.jsoup.nodes.Document;

public class HtmlTagFixer {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        String html = "<html><body><h1>Hello World</h1><p>This is a paragraph.</p></body></html>";
        
        // Find and replace all occurrences of specific HTML tags
        Document doc = Jsoup.parse(html);
        for (org.jsoup.nodes.Document tag : doc.select("h1,p")) {
            String newText = replaceTags(tag);
            tag.text(newText);
        }
    }

    public static String replaceTags(org.jsoup.nodes.Document tag) {
        return tag.html().replaceAll("<[^>]*>", "");
    }
}
```
This code uses the `select` method to find all occurrences of the `h1` and `p` tags, and then calls the `replaceTags` method to replace each occurrence with a new text string. The `replaceAll` method is used to replace all occurrences of specific HTML tags (in this case, `<[^>]*>` matches any HTML tag).
